WEBVTT

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.967
Welcome back, creators! Our game looks amazing.

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.900
But try clicking a card...

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.267
Nothing happens. In fact, there are no cards yet!

00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.300
Looks alone don't make a game.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.233
A game needs to respond, to remember, and to decide.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.467
That's the job of JavaScript.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:33.700
JavaScript gives our game its behaviour.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.100
You can think of it as the game's brain.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.433
Click the new file button,

00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:43.967
type script.js, and press Enter.

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:48.200
Our project now has three files.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:51.500
Back in index.html, under the

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.400
stylesheet link, we add a <script> tag.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.133
Its src attribute says script.js.

00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:02.367
The word defer means: first finish reading the

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:05.867
whole page, and then run the JavaScript.

00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:08.067
That way, every element is ready

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.833
when our code looks for it.

00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:16.033
Let's meet our first big idea: variables.

00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:18.600
A variable is like a labelled box,

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.800
where the computer keeps information.

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:25.533
let moves = 0; creates a box called

00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:28.600
moves, and puts zero inside.

00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:32.367
Later, we can change what's inside.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:34.133
When the player makes a move,

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.567
moves becomes one, then two, then three.

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:43.967
Our game uses variables to remember everything important.

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:47.533
firstCard and secondCard remember the two

00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:50.100
cards the player has turned over.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.433
They start as null, which means empty.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:58.067
lockBoard remembers whether the player has to wait.

00:01:58.433 --> 00:02:01.233
moves and matchedPairs keep count.

00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:04.333
totalPairs remembers how many pairs we

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:07.533
need, and seconds counts the time.

00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:11.967
Next big idea: arrays.

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.833
An array stores several values together,

00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:18.600
in order, inside square brackets.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.667
Here, the numbers array holds one,

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:26.300
two, three, four, five and six.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.167
But a memory game needs pairs!

00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:32.700
So our createDeck function uses the numbers

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.067
twice, joined into one bigger array.

00:02:36.433 --> 00:02:42.867
Now every number has a partner. Twelve cards, six pairs.

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:47.600
Big idea number three: functions.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.467
A function is a set of instructions with a name.

00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:55.667
We write it once, and use it again and again.

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:59.300
This function is called sayHello.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.667
When we call it, it writes "Hello, creator!"

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.567
in the console.

00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:10.000
Our game has lots of helpful functions.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.933
createDeck makes the pairs of numbers.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:17.033
shuffle mixes them into a random order,

00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:19.833
just like shuffling real cards.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:22.033
createCard builds one card,

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:25.633
with its green back and its number front.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.000
buildBoard puts all the cards into the game grid.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.700
And flipCard turns a card over when the player clicks it.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.700
But how does the game know when we click? With events.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.800
An event is something that happens on a webpage,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.600
like a click, a key press, or a choice from a list.

00:03:48.967 --> 00:03:52.800
This line tells each card: listen for a click.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.633
And when a click happens, run flipCard for this card.

00:03:58.033 --> 00:04:03.933
Click... and flipCard runs. The card turns over!

00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:08.367
Our game also needs to make decisions.

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:12.200
For that, we use if, and else.

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:14.667
This if statement asks a question:

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:19.467
is the number on the first card the same as the number on the second card?

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:21.467
If the answer is yes,

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:25.400
the cards match, and they stay face up.

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:27.800
Else, if the answer is no,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.633
they don't match, and they turn back over.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.933
Three equals signs mean: are these exactly the same?

00:04:37.933 --> 00:04:41.067
One more idea: the DOM.

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:43.800
That's how JavaScript sees our webpage:

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.167
as a family of elements it can find, and change.

00:04:48.533 --> 00:04:53.667
document.getElementById finds an element using its id.

00:04:53.667 --> 00:04:58.367
Here, it finds the game grid we made in HTML.

00:04:58.567 --> 00:05:04.233
Remember when we gave our elements ids? This is why!

00:05:04.633 --> 00:05:08.533
Then, createElement makes a brand new button for each

00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:12.200
card, and appendChild adds it into the grid.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.900
That's how the cards appear.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.233
JavaScript builds them for us!

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.433
Your game now has a brain!

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.500
We have learned about variables,

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:30.300
arrays, functions, events, decisions, and the DOM.

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:32.867
But we haven't connected all those ideas

00:05:32.867 --> 00:05:35.967
into the complete game just yet.

00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:39.400
Let's do that in our next lesson!
